933 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 449 0 R
/T (Explain the three file allocation methods \(contiguous, linked, indexed\) in detail. )
/E (Explain the three file allocation methods \(contiguous, linked, indexed\) in detail. )
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
934 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1921 0 R
/E (Contiguous: Files stored in consecutive blocks. Sequential access is fastest; random access is also fast \(direct calculation\). Suffers from external fragmentation; file size must be known in advance.)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
935 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1922 0 R
/E (Linked: Files stored as linked blocks via pointers. No external fragmentation; files can grow dynamically. Random access is very slow \(O\(n\) traversal\); pointers consume space in each block.)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
936 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1923 0 R
/E (Indexed: An index block stores pointers to all data blocks. Fast random access \(O\(1\) via index\); no external fragmentation. Large files need multiple index blocks \(linked index or multi-level index\).)
/Pg 60 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
