/E (Discuss different directory structures \(single-level, two-level, tree-structured, acyclic-graph\). Explain their advantages and disadvantages.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
941 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1927 0 R
/E (Single-Level: One directory for all files. Simple to implement; but causes naming conflicts and is unmanageable with many files.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
942 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1928 0 R
/E (Two-Level: One directory per user. Solves naming conflicts between users; but files cannot be logically grouped within a user's directory.)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
943 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1929 0 R
/E <>
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
944 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1930 0 R
/E (Acyclic-Graph: Allows shared subdirectories/files via links \(like Unix symlinks\). Enables sharing without duplication; but deletion is complex \(reference counting or garbage collection needed\).)
/Pg 61 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
